  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The last prayer before the consecration, asking that the offering be made the Body and Blood. Its five adjectives are the crux: BENEDÍCTAM at w008, RATAM at w010, RATIONÁBILEM at w011 and ACCEPTABILÉMQUE at w012 are adjectives, ADSCRÍPTAM at w009 a perfect passive participle of adscríbo — not an inconsistency of ours but of the dictionaries' own drawing: Lewis and Short gives ratus a participial-adjective head with senses of its own and adscriptus a bare participle head, and each token follows the evidence that exists for it. FIAT at w020 is tagged active with the corpus's earlier rulings on fio, whose forms are active and whose sense is passive. CORPUS and SANGUIS at w017 and w019 stand in the nominative though everything around them stands in other cases — genitives and a dative: this edition reads them as predicate nominatives, what the offering is to become, with the offering itself the unspoken singular subject of fiat. That is why the verb is singular, and why both translations say that it may become. Read instead as a compound subject they would want a plural, and the singular would be the exception the grammars allow.",
    "source": {
